# Part 2 – Updating and Migrating (Cindy 0.1.120 → 0.1.121)

After using the initial installation, I attempted to update Cindy through its built-in updater.

The application displayed the following message:

> This installation cannot update itself. Use your package manager, or follow the Linux user-install guide.

The message also explained that a managed installation might require missing tools or restored write access.

Instead of continuing with the manually extracted application, I decided to migrate to Cindy's bundled managed user-installation method.

## 5. Inspect the Built-in Linux Installation Scripts

The original Debian package contained Cindy's own Linux installation scripts:

```text
usr/lib/cindy/resources/linux/install-user.sh
usr/lib/cindy/resources/linux/register-desktop.sh
```

These scripts were also included in the newer 0.1.121 package.

## 6. Install the Required Fedora Dependency

The user installer required `bsdtar`, which was not initially available on my system.

I checked which Fedora package provided it:

```bash
sudo dnf provides '*/bsdtar'
```

Then installed it:

```bash
sudo dnf install bsdtar
```

I also checked for the other required utilities:

```bash
for x in bsdtar sha256sum stat dd mktemp realpath flock find readlink mv ln desktop-file-validate update-desktop-database xdg-mime; do
    command -v "$x" || echo "MISSING: $x"
done
```

All required utilities were available after installing `bsdtar`.

## 7. Check the Official Update Manifest

I queried Cindy's official Linux update manifest:

```bash
curl -fsSL https://hotfix.cindy.app/cindy/manifest-linux-x64.json \
| python3 -c 'import json,sys; d=json.load(sys.stdin); print(json.dumps({"version":d["app"]["version"],"installer":d["app"]["installer"]},indent=2))'
```

The manifest identified the following release:

```text
Version: 0.1.121

Installer:
app/linux-x64/cindy-0.1.121-amd64.deb

SHA-256:
b756663c3f30d6cf8a75d4d8ea38336842689a504b682d62c6dd3d6ba01e0663

Size:
199686896 bytes
```

The manifest provided the download information and checksum for the new release.

## 8. Download Cindy 0.1.121

Download the new Debian package:

```bash
cd ~/Downloads

curl -fL \
  https://hotfix.cindy.app/cindy/app/linux-x64/cindy-0.1.121-amd64.deb \
  -o cindy-0.1.121-amd64.deb
```

## 9. Verify the Downloaded Package

Before running the installer, I checked the downloaded package's SHA-256 checksum:

```bash
sha256sum cindy-0.1.121-amd64.deb
```

Expected checksum:

```text
b756663c3f30d6cf8a75d4d8ea38336842689a504b682d62c6dd3d6ba01e0663
```

**The calculated SHA-256 checksum matched the value published in Cindy's official update manifest.**

Always verify a package against the trusted checksum for that exact release. Do not reuse an old checksum for a newer version.

## 10. Extract the Verified Package

I extracted the verified package to inspect the installation scripts:

```bash
mkdir -p ~/Downloads/cindy-0.1.121-inspect

dpkg-deb -x ~/Downloads/cindy-0.1.121-amd64.deb \
  ~/Downloads/cindy-0.1.121-inspect
```

Check the bundled Linux installation scripts:

```bash
ls -l ~/Downloads/cindy-0.1.121-inspect/usr/lib/cindy/resources/linux/
```

The package contained:

```text
install-omarchy.sh
install-user.sh
register-desktop.sh
```

The build information confirmed the version and architecture:

```bash
cat ~/Downloads/cindy-0.1.121-inspect/usr/lib/cindy/resources/linux-build-info
```

Output:

```text
cindy-linux-v1
0.1.121
x64
global
Cindy
```

For this Fedora installation, I used `install-user.sh`, not the Omarchy-specific installer.

## 11. Back Up Existing User Data

Before migrating, close Cindy completely.

It is recommended to back up the existing configuration and user data before proceeding.

On my system, the relevant locations included:

```text
~/.config/CindyGlobal
~/.config/Cindy
~/.local/share/Cindy
```

An example backup command:

```bash
BACKUP="$HOME/Downloads/cindy-backup-$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S).tar.gz"

tar -czf "$BACKUP" \
  -C "$HOME" \
  .config/CindyGlobal \
  .config/Cindy \
  .local/share/Cindy

echo "Backup created at: $BACKUP"
ls -lh "$BACKUP"
```

Your actual profile locations may differ. Check which directories exist before using the command.

Keep the backup until you have verified the new installation and your existing data.

## 12. Install Cindy Using the Official User Installer

I used the `install-user.sh` script bundled with the verified 0.1.121 package.

I selected a separate installation directory to keep the original manually extracted installation untouched:

```bash
bash ~/Downloads/cindy-0.1.121-inspect/usr/lib/cindy/resources/linux/install-user.sh \
  --install ~/Downloads/cindy-0.1.121-amd64.deb \
  b756663c3f30d6cf8a75d4d8ea38336842689a504b682d62c6dd3d6ba01e0663 \
  "$HOME/.local/opt/cindy-managed"
```

The installer completed successfully:

```text
Installed Cindy 0.1.121.
Start with: /home/lporcsin/.local/opt/cindy-managed/launch
```

The new managed installation was created under:

```text
~/.local/opt/cindy-managed
```

**Important:**

- Run the user installer as your regular desktop user.
- Do not use `sudo` for this installation.
- Do not run the old and new installations simultaneously against the same user profile.
- Keep the original installation until the new one has been verified.

## 13. Register the New GNOME Application Launcher

The new installation included its own desktop-registration script.

I registered Cindy in GNOME with:

```bash
bash "$HOME/.local/opt/cindy-managed/current/resources/linux/register-desktop.sh" \
  "$HOME/.local/opt/cindy-managed"
```

Then launched the managed installation:

```bash
"$HOME/.local/opt/cindy-managed/launch"
```

**Result:** Cindy 0.1.121 launched successfully from the managed user installation.

## 14. Remove the Duplicate GNOME Launcher

After registering the new installation, GNOME displayed two Cindy icons:

- **Cindy** – the old manually created launcher pointing to the extracted application.
- **Cindy (User)** – the new launcher registered by Cindy's managed user installation.

The duplicate appeared because the old custom desktop entry was still present.

After verifying the new installation, I removed the obsolete desktop entry:

```bash
rm ~/.local/share/applications/cindy.desktop
```

This removes only the old launcher file, not the application profile or user data.

I kept the new **Cindy (User)** launcher.

If the old icon remains visible temporarily, refresh the GNOME session or log out and back in.

